  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 332 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nLittle change in the forecast for the long term. While some \nimportant things in the atmosphere will be happening around the \nperiphery of the southeast across the next week, the sensible \nimpacts to north and central Georgia will be to keep things sunny \nand mostly mild with no chances for precipitation.\n\nUpper level low that has been mostly cut off from the upper jet will \ncontinue to spin off the southeastern coastline to start the long \nterm into Monday night. A surface low may form within this, with the \nprimary impact being to continue our E to ENE wind direction that \nkeeps the mild air in place. A stronger upper level trough will dive \nthrough the NE during this time period, pushing another surface high \npressure across the northern Appalachians. This will settle into \nplace there by Wednesday, pushing air down the Appalachians once \nagain that will just reinforce our current air mass. All in all, \nthis means repeated days of highs in the 70s and lows in the 40s to \nupper 50s across the entire CWA, with little to no chance of rain.\n\nAs we move towards Halloween, there is some signal of things being a \ntouch warmer, but uncertainty remains. Model spread is upwards of 10 \ndegrees for afternoon highs on Friday and Saturday, in part due to \nuncertainty around the position and timing of a wave over the Plains \nthat could pull in warm air across much of the eastern half of the \ncountry. Stay tuned, but current forecast would put temperatures in",
